<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<suppressions xmlns="https://jeremylong.github.io/DependencyCheck/dependency-suppression.1.3.xsd">
	<!--
	  SELF-CPE FALSE POSITIVE (systemic, narrowed 2026-08-05): our own first-party sh.stubborn:*
	  jars keep getting matched to third-party PRODUCT CPEs purely on artifactId/description name
	  tokens plus the low 0.x version, inheriting those products' historical CVEs (e.g. Spring Boot
	  CVE-2017-8046, CVE-2021-26987, CVE-2023-20873, CVE-2023-20883, CVE-2022-27772,
	  CVE-2026-40972/40975/22733; WireMock CVE-2018-9116; legacy Spring Framework CVEs). None of our
	  jars ARE those products.
	  Previously this rule suppressed ALL CPE matches (<cpe>.*</cpe>) for any sh.stubborn artifact,
	  at any version — broad enough to mask a genuine future CVE that happens to arrive under a CPE.
	  Narrowed to (a) the specific mis-attributing third-party product vendors actually observed
	  (vmware / pivotal / springsource spring products + wiremock), and (b) the pre-1.0 0.x line
	  only. A CVE against our own code (matched by packageUrl/CVE, or under any other vendor CPE)
	  is now evaluated, and the whole rule re-opens for review at 1.0.
	  REVIEW/REMOVE at the 1.0 release, or sooner if OSS-Index/NVD stops the name-token mis-match.
	-->
	<suppress>
		<notes><![CDATA[ First-party sh.stubborn 0.x artifacts are matched to third-party spring/wiremock product CPEs purely on name tokens + the low version, inheriting those products' CVEs. Our own jars are never those products. Scoped to the observed mis-attributing vendors (vmware/pivotal/springsource/wiremock) and the 0.x line only, so genuine CVEs (matched by packageUrl/CVE or any other CPE) stay evaluated and the rule re-opens at 1.0. Narrowed 2026-08-05. ]]></notes>
		<packageUrl regex="true">^pkg:maven/sh\.stubborn/.*@0\..*$</packageUrl>
		<cpe regex="true">^cpe:/a:(vmware|pivotal_software|pivotal|springsource|wiremock):.*$</cpe>
	</suppress>

	<!--
	  CVE-2026-53914 (CVSS 9.8) — JetBrains Kotlin unsafe deserialization, fixed on the Kotlin
	  2.4.20 line. Kotlin reaches this build only as build-time / DSL tooling, in two modules:
	    * stubborn-contract-migration pulls it transitively via OpenRewrite (rewrite-gradle -> rewrite-kotlin).
	      rewrite-gradle is retained for the planned Gradle build-script migration recipes.
	    * stubborn-contract-extras (the Kotlin contract DSL) gets Kotlin from the Spring Boot 4.1 BOM (2.3.21)
	      plus explicit kotlin-scripting-* pins (2.2.21) — its own language runtime.
	  Neither can be realigned to the fix line today: Kotlin 2.4.20 is not yet on Maven Central as GA
	  (only 2.4.20-Beta2 is published; the stable line tops out at 2.4.10, still below the fix), and we
	  do not ship a pre-release Kotlin. Both are build-time/DSL only — the vulnerable deserialization
	  path is not reachable from any runtime artifact we publish.
	  REMOVE THIS SUPPRESSION and align every org.jetbrains.kotlin artifact to >= 2.4.20 once GA ships.
	-->
	<suppress>
		<notes><![CDATA[
			Build-time/DSL Kotlin only (stubborn-contract-migration via OpenRewrite; stubborn-contract-extras Kotlin DSL).
			Kotlin 2.4.20 (the fix) is not yet GA on Maven Central. Remove once every
			org.jetbrains.kotlin artifact can be pinned to >= 2.4.20 GA.
		]]></notes>
		<packageUrl regex="true">^pkg:maven/org\.jetbrains\.kotlin/.*$</packageUrl>
		<cve>CVE-2026-53914</cve>
	</suppress>

	<!--
	  CVE-2026-66299 (CVSS 7.5) — Apache Tomcat embedded, flagged on tomcat-embed-core 11.0.24.
	  No fixed Tomcat 11.0.x is published on Maven Central yet (11.0.24 is the latest; the pin in
	  stubborn-contract-dependencies is already at that ceiling), so there is nothing to bump to.
	  Reachability is build-time / test only, never a consumer's runtime:
	    * stubborn-contract-maven-plugin pulls spring-boot-starter-web at COMPILE scope, but that
	      runs only inside the producer's build (plugin realm), not the shipped app.
	    * stubborn-contract-stub-runner-spring-cloud declares starter-web at TEST scope.
	    * stubborn-contract-stub-runner-spring-boot declares starter-web as <optional>true</optional>,
	      so Tomcat is NOT transitive to consumers of that artifact.
	  The stub runner serves HTTP stubs via WireMock/Jetty, not Tomcat — the embedded servlet
	  container is never on the stub-serving path.
	  REMOVE THIS SUPPRESSION and bump ${tomcat.version} once Apache publishes a fixed Tomcat
	  11.0.x (11.0.25+).
	-->
	<suppress>
		<notes><![CDATA[
			Tomcat embedded reaches this build only as a build-time (maven-plugin) / test-scope /
			optional dependency — never a consumer runtime dep — and the stub runner serves over
			WireMock/Jetty, not Tomcat. No fixed Tomcat 11.0.x is on Central yet (11.0.24 is latest).
			Remove and bump the pin once 11.0.25+ ships.
		]]></notes>
		<packageUrl regex="true">^pkg:maven/org\.apache\.tomcat\.embed/.*$</packageUrl>
		<cve>CVE-2026-66299</cve>
	</suppress>

	<!--
	  CVE-2025-67721 (CVSS 6.3) — io.airlift:aircompressor 0.27. Pulled in only as a
	  build-time transitive of the Maven plugin's Avro/Confluent toolchain
	  (io.confluent:kafka-avro-serializer -> avro -> aircompressor); it is not on any
	  consumer's runtime classpath. The score (6.3) is below the project's
	  failBuildOnCVSS=7.0 policy — the gate trips only because dependency-check has no
	  CVSSv3 score for this CVE and treats the unscored record as failing. The version is
	  set by the transitive chain, not by us. REMOVE once the Avro/Confluent chain resolves
	  a fixed aircompressor, or once the CVE carries a scored-below-7.0 record.
	-->
	<suppress>
		<notes><![CDATA[
			aircompressor is a build-time-only transitive of the Maven plugin's Avro/Confluent
			toolchain, never a consumer runtime dependency. CVSS 6.3 (below the 7.0 fail policy);
			flagged only because the CVE has no CVSSv3 score. Remove when the transitive chain
			updates aircompressor.
		]]></notes>
		<packageUrl regex="true">^pkg:maven/io\.airlift/aircompressor@.*$</packageUrl>
		<cve>CVE-2025-67721</cve>
	</suppress>

	<!--
	  FALSE POSITIVE: org.apache.camel:camel-kafka is the Camel *Kafka component* (the Camel
	  connector for Kafka), NOT Apache Kafka itself. dependency-check auto-generates the CPE
	  cpe:2.3:a:apache:kafka for it because of the "apache" + "kafka" tokens, so every
	  apache:kafka broker CVE gets matched against camel-kafka's Camel version number (4.21.0),
	  which is not even a real Apache Kafka release. None of the Apache Kafka broker/client CVEs
	  apply to this artifact. Suppress ONLY the apache:kafka CPE, scoped to the camel-kafka
	  packageUrl, so genuine apache:camel CVEs on this artifact are still evaluated (they are
	  handled by keeping camel.version current) and real Apache Kafka dependencies elsewhere in
	  the reactor are unaffected. This is a structural mismatch, not version-dependent - keep.
	-->
	<suppress>
		<notes><![CDATA[
			camel-kafka is the Apache Camel Kafka *component*, not Apache Kafka. The apache:kafka
			CPE is a mis-generated match on the "apache"/"kafka" name tokens; camel-kafka's version
			(4.21.0) is a Camel version, not a Kafka version, so no Apache Kafka CVE applies.
		]]></notes>
		<packageUrl regex="true">^pkg:maven/org\.apache\.camel/camel-kafka@.*$</packageUrl>
		<cpe>cpe:/a:apache:kafka</cpe>
	</suppress>

	<!--
	  ADJACENT-CPE FALSE POSITIVE: io.github.x-stream:mxparser is the XML pull-parser fork that
	  XStream bundles (an MXP1/XPP3 successor), NOT XStream itself. dependency-check matches it to
	  the XStream CPE (cpe:/a:x-stream:xstream, a.k.a. cpe:/a:xstream:xstream) on the "x-stream"
	  groupId token and assigns it XStream's own deserialization CVEs — CVE-2020-26217 (8.8),
	  CVE-2021-39144 (8.5), CVE-2021-39145/39146/39147/39148/39149. Those CVEs belong to the
	  com.thoughtworks.xstream:xstream engine, not to the parser. The real XStream jar on this
	  reactor's classpath is already at the fixed 1.4.21 (>= 1.4.21 remediates every one of those
	  CVEs; it reaches only stubborn-contract-stub-runner-spring-cloud transitively via Curator),
	  so nothing is actually exploitable — the parser fork merely inherits the CPE. Suppress ONLY
	  the xstream CPEs, scoped to the mxparser packageUrl, so a genuine CVE filed against mxparser
	  itself (matched some other way) is still surfaced and the real xstream jar is untouched.
	  Structural name-token mismatch, not version-dependent — keep.
	-->
	<suppress>
		<notes><![CDATA[
			io.github.x-stream:mxparser is XStream's bundled XML pull-parser fork, not XStream.
			It is CPE-matched to cpe:/a:x-stream:xstream on the "x-stream" groupId token and assigned
			XStream's deserialization CVEs (CVE-2020-26217, CVE-2021-39144..39149), which belong to
			the com.thoughtworks.xstream:xstream engine (present at the fixed 1.4.21 here), not the
			parser. Only the xstream CPEs are suppressed, scoped to this artifact.
		]]></notes>
		<packageUrl regex="true">^pkg:maven/io\.github\.x-stream/mxparser@.*$</packageUrl>
		<cpe>cpe:/a:x-stream:xstream</cpe>
		<cpe>cpe:/a:xstream:xstream</cpe>
	</suppress>

	<!--
	  DOMPurify — the DOMPurify JavaScript library vendored inside the swagger-ui bundle
	  (swagger-ui-bundle.js / swagger-ui-es-bundle.js, pkg:javascript/DOMPurify) that WireMock
	  3.13.2 ships inside wiremock-*.jar for its standalone admin UI.
	  This is a browser-only front-end asset: it is exploitable only when a user loads WireMock's
	  standalone admin swagger-ui in a browser. Stubborn Contract embeds WireMock purely as an
	  in-process HTTP stub server (verifier / wiremock modules) and never serves that swagger-ui,
	  so the vulnerable code path is not reachable from any artifact we publish. It is also outside
	  our control — the fix must come from an upstream WireMock release bundling a patched DOMPurify.
	  Suppressed by packageUrl WITHOUT a <cve> filter on purpose: the vendored DOMPurify version
	  attracts a rolling set of low-severity DOM-clobbering/XSS CVEs (the original CVE-2026-65898 no
	  longer matches; ODC now reports CVE-2026-49978, CVE-2026-49458, CVE-2026-49459, CVE-2026-41240,
	  CVE-2026-0540, CVE-2026-65902, CVE-2026-65914 and more, currently <7 but churning). A
	  CVE-pinned rule would rot into a dead "zero matches" entry on every WireMock bump; a bounded
	  version range keeps the browser-only asset suppressed regardless of the exact CVE while still
	  self-expiring on the next bump.
	  NARROWED 2026-08-05: previously scoped to ANY DOMPurify version (@.*), broad enough to mask a
	  future CVE against a different — possibly reachable — DOMPurify build. WireMock 3.13.2 vendors
	  exactly DOMPurify 3.2.6 (verified: swagger-ui-dist/swagger-ui-bundle.js contains
	  DOMPurify.version="3.2.6"), so the rule is pinned to that exact version. The moment WireMock
	  ships a different DOMPurify the suppression stops matching and the finding re-surfaces.
	  REMOVE (or re-pin) once WireMock ships a swagger-ui bundle with a patched DOMPurify.
	-->
	<suppress>
		<notes><![CDATA[
			Browser-only DOMPurify 3.2.6 inside WireMock 3.13.2's vendored swagger-ui admin bundle.
			WireMock is used only as an in-process stub server here; the swagger-ui is never served,
			so the XSS path is unreachable at runtime. Upstream-owned, browser-only asset. Pinned to
			the exact vendored version (3.2.6) so any WireMock/DOMPurify bump re-surfaces the finding.
			Narrowed 2026-08-05. Remove once WireMock bundles a patched DOMPurify.
		]]></notes>
		<packageUrl regex="true">^pkg:javascript/DOMPurify@3\.2\.6$</packageUrl>
		<vulnerabilityName regex="true">.*</vulnerabilityName>
	</suppress>

	<!--
	  NOTE: the previously separate per-module self-CPE suppressions —
	    * sh.stubborn *wiremock* jars -> cpe:/a:wiremock:wiremock (CVE-2018-9116)
	    * sh.stubborn:stubborn-contract-converters -> cpe:/a:vmware:* (legacy Spring CVEs)
	  have been folded into the single (narrowed 2026-08-05) first-party self-CPE rule at the top
	  of this file.
	-->
</suppressions>
